The MCP panel listed a server that cannot work here
===================================================

MCP is not a macOS feature and there is no Linux equivalent to look for — it is JSON-RPC, and this
build serves it on Linux: `quixi-mcp-builtin --computer-use` answers an `initialize` and lists 44
tools, `--fetch` identifies itself as `quixi-fetch 3.6556.12`. What was actually wrong was narrower.

`applescript` is a macOS capability, and the seeding loop already knows that — it only adds the
adapter when `cfg!(target_os = "macos")`. But it only stopped *seeding* it. The entry was already
written to `~/.quixi/quixi_desktop_config.json` by an earlier run, so on this box the panel showed
seven servers, `applescript` among them, and every call against that one failed. On a machine where
the agent is being judged by what it can do, one broken row reads as a broken protocol.

The existing config is now swept on startup: on anything that is not macOS, a `quixi-mcp-builtin`
entry carrying `--applescript` is removed. Only the built-in adapter is touched — a server the user
defined is never pruned, even if it is itself called `applescript`, because that is their
configuration and not ours to edit.

Measured after: six servers, every one of which resolves to a tool that exists on this platform.

